Skip the stack map search for every verified instruction.

Before each instruction the verifier asks check_current_target() for the
stack map at its bci, and the answer was found by a search: the cache for
the first 512 bytes of a method, a binary search past it. Most instructions
have no stack map, and a method of several kilobytes searched the maps for
every instruction past 512 bytes. The caller, bytecode_prolog(), already
walks the stack maps in step with the instructions to check their order.

When the stack maps of the method have increasing bcis, which
populate_stackmap_cache() checks, an instruction has a stack map exactly
when bytecode_prolog() has just passed one, and the index is the one it
passed: the maps before it were passed at smaller bcis and the ones after it
have larger bcis than the next one, which is not smaller than the bci. Hand
that index (or none) to check_current_target(), which now returns at once
for an instruction without a map that follows control flow, and otherwise
matches the frame exactly as before. An instruction without a map that
follows an unconditional transfer still fails with the same error, and
methods with unordered or repeated maps keep the search.

Also hash symbol names four characters per loop iteration. The value is the
same recurrence, so symbols in the ROM table and in the heap table are found
as before.

Loading every class of three games with the JIT off took 2.9 to 3.9 percent
fewer instructions, the verifier functions 11 to 12 percent fewer; the classes
loaded at start-up alone took 1.4 percent fewer.

